        "geology": "The geology at Niwot Ridge consists of schist, migmatite, and biotitic gneiss.",
        "hydrology": "NIWO is located within the Rocky Mountains, which are covered in snowmelt-fed springs and upland catchments which deliver water to downstream basins. Boulder receives most of its drinking water from these basins.",
        "soils": "The dominate soil order found at NIWO are Mollisols. Overall, soil can be described as Coarse-loamy - mixed - superactive Typic Haplocryolls.",
        "vegetation": "Flora varies along an elevation gradient at NIWO. On the low-elevation east and south facing slopes, lodgepole pine (Pinus contorta) and aspen (Populus tremuloides) groves dominate the landscape. The transition zone between the closed subalpine forest and alpine tundra are full of meadows interspersed with stunted patches of coniferous trees. The alpine tundra sits above the treeline and is dominated by herbaceous plants such as Ross’ avens (Geum rossii) and curly sedge (Carex rupestris). The steepest, highest regions of NIWO are dominated by microplants and scree."
      },
